// Template: iterative scientific-research consensus with a variable
// roster of providers. One panel shape, two uses:
//
//   - de-novo research: the anchor file is a *problem statement*
//     (data references, prior results, constraints, success criteria);
//   - research-plan refinement: the anchor file is a *draft research
//     plan* whose hypotheses, methodology, evidence bars, milestones,
//     and stopping rules the panel strengthens.
//
// This file supersedes scientific_plan_refinement.gaviero — the prompts
// tell reviewers to detect which kind of anchor they were given; the
// artefact contract is identical either way.
//
// Shape:
//
//   Phase 1 (parallel, one per reviewer): each reviewer reads the
//     anchor document ({{PLAN_FILE}}) plus any optional local material
//     under {{DATA_DIR}} / {{LOCAL_LIT_DIR}}, performs extensive
//     independent literature research (Semantic Scholar MCP and/or web
//     tools — see "Research channels"), and writes (a) a standalone
//     conclusion document carrying its own bibliography and (b) a
//     production summary. Reviewers do NOT read each other's work in
//     this phase — the point is an independent baseline.
//
//   Phase 2 (loop up to 10): each reviewer reads every peer's prior
//     conclusion + summary, performs ADDITIONAL targeted literature
//     research, and writes a new conclusion and a new production
//     summary. After each pass `convergence-judge`
//     (sonnet) classifies the iteration; the loop exits on PASS after
//     `stability` consecutive passes, or hits `max_iterations 10`.
//
// Execution mode:
//
//   `execution_mode document` — one shared working directory, no git
//   worktrees, no branch merge, no repo-map. This is load-bearing, not
//   a preference: refine agents read every peer's prior conclusion
//   and summary, and worktrees are merged only *after* the
//   loop exits. Under the default `repo` mode each reviewer would see
//   an empty {{OUT_DIR}}/ and every refine pass would silently degrade
//   to a solo pass with no error raised.
//
//   Parallelism is unaffected: each reviewer's `owned` globs are
//   prefixed with its own {{REVIEWER_ID}}, so the scope enforcer keeps
//   concurrent writes disjoint. Never drop that id prefix.
//
// Anchor precedence:
//
//   {{PLAN_FILE}} is the run's ONLY source of truth. It alone fixes
//   what the panel studies.
//
//   `--prompt` ({{PROMPT}}) is a run label: a short human-readable
//   string for logs, agent descriptions, and memory entries. It has
//   no authority. When the label and the anchor appear to name
//   different subjects, the anchor wins — every prompt in this file
//   says so explicitly, because a reviewer left to resolve it alone
//   will not resolve it the same way as its peers.
//
//   That is not hypothetical. A run whose label was a CS-education
//   topic and whose anchor was a software feature brief produced two
//   conclusions about two different subjects: one reviewer read the
//   label as authoritative (on the ground that the anchor asked for
//   an implementation roadmap these rules forbid), the other read the
//   anchor as authoritative and forced it into the nearest permitted
//   category. Both diagnosed the mismatch correctly; neither could
//   converge with the other, because they were not studying the same
//   thing. Two frontier iterations went into arguing about which CLI
//   argument wins.
//
//   An anchor the panel cannot use is a reportable defect, never a
//   licence to substitute a subject. Reviewers name the defect in
//   their summary's `## Problems found` and analyse what the anchor
//   does name; the judge returns `irreconcilable` with
//   `criterion: framing` the first time it sees a split subject or an
//   unusable anchor, so the run stops after one iteration with an
//   actionable hand-off instead of burning the panel's budget.
//
//   Practical consequence: pick the anchor first and let the label
//   describe it. A software-engineering brief (implementation plan,
//   PR decomposition, module ownership) is not a valid anchor for
//   this workflow at all — its Hard rules forbid producing that
//   artefact. Use a plan-refinement workflow for those.
//
// Anchoring (two ways to run — omit `--repo` in both):
//
//   With `--workspace <dir>` (research layout): {{PLAN_FILE}} and
//   {{OUT_DIR}} are relative to that directory; artefacts collect
//   under {{OUT_DIR}} (default `research/`).
//
//   With `--var PLAN_FILE=/abs/path.md` and no `--workspace`
//   (plan-refinement layout): gaviero-cli special-cases the PLAN_FILE
//   var name — it anchors the workspace to the plan's folder and
//   defaults OUT_DIR there, so the versioned panel artefacts land
//   alongside the plan. Pass `--var OUT_DIR=...` to redirect them.
//
// Research channels:
//
//   Reviewers prefer the operator-configured Semantic Scholar MCP
//   server (id {{SEMANTIC_SCHOLAR_SERVER}}) when one is available and
//   use WebSearch/WebFetch when ENABLE_WEB_FALLBACK=true (the default)
//   or when no MCP server is configured. Set
//   `--var ENABLE_WEB_FALLBACK=false` for an MCP-only panel; a
//   reviewer left without any working channel must say so in its
//   summary and rely on local material.
//
//   Configure the MCP server any one of these ways:
//     --mcp-url semantic-scholar=URL
//     --var SEMANTIC_SCHOLAR_MCP_URL=URL
//     a server entry in <workspace>/.cursor/mcp.json (gaviero-cli
//     imports it).
//   (Wuilder URLs need a trailing slash; gaviero-cli adds it for
//   mcp.wuilder.com.) Cursor agents get `--approve-mcps` and
//   `--sandbox disabled` plus `.cursor/cli.json` network/MCP
//   allowlists when a remote Semantic Scholar URL is configured; the
//   synthesized Cursor `.cursor/mcp.json` omits context7 (npx) so the
//   remote server can register. Agents call tools by server id
//   `{{SEMANTIC_SCHOLAR_SERVER}}` (default `semantic-scholar`).
//
// Roster:
//
//   The reviewer panel is a workflow parameter — supplied via
//   `--param roster=...`. The in-script default below runs a
//   three-provider frontier panel at maximum research depth:
//   Claude Opus + Codex GPT-5.6 Sol + Cursor Grok 4.5. Each roster
//   entry is `id=provider:model[@effort]`; the id becomes the filename
//   prefix and the model + effort bind the cloned reviewer agent to an
//   anonymous client. Use distinct ids per reviewer.
//
//   Cursor note: the Cursor backend does not forward `effort`;
//   reasoning depth rides on the model id itself. `cursor:grok-4.5`
//   is the canonical id (see clients.gaviero) — check variants with
//   `agent --list-models` before inventing suffixes.
//
//   The judge stays on `claude:sonnet` at `medium` — it only
//   classifies summaries against a fixed rubric and runs once per
//   iteration, so raising its effort multiplies cost without
//   improving the verdict.
//
// Files written per iteration N (N=1 for init, N=2..11 for refinement):
//
//   {{OUT_DIR}}/<id>-conclusion-v{N}.md      (the deliverable)
//   {{OUT_DIR}}/<id>-summary-v{N}.md         (the production log)
//
//   Exactly two files, every iteration including init. Artefacts are
//   write-once: create each versioned file exactly once, read it
//   later, never overwrite a prior version. They are scratch outputs,
//   not meant to be committed.
//
//   Both templates declare these paths in a `produces [...]` block, so
//   the contract is enforced by the runtime rather than trusted to the
//   prompt. Two checks, neither of which costs a model call:
//
//     - Per agent: a reviewer that ends its turn without writing both
//       files (or writes an empty one) gets one corrective retry and
//       then a Failed manifest — instead of "completed, 0 files".
//     - Per iteration: the loop refuses to invoke `convergence-judge`
//       unless every reviewer delivered this pass, and aborts the run
//       naming the reviewer and the missing paths. A judge scoring a
//       panel that silently lost a member returns a meaningless
//       verdict and burns the whole iteration budget doing it.
//
//   `{{ITER}}` in a `produces` path is substituted per pass, so writing
//   the previous version does not satisfy the contract.
//
// Termination:
//
//   The run is finite by construction, not by hoping every agent
//   returns. Three independent bounds, none of which costs a model
//   call:
//
//     1. `timeout 3600` on each reviewer template caps one dispatch.
//        This is the load-bearing one. Provider sessions only give up
//        when their subprocess *exits*; a wedged-but-alive CLI keeps
//        the idle loop spinning forever, which is exactly how a run
//        freezes after the last iteration. With a per-dispatch cap the
//        worst case is (1 + max_iterations) x (reviewers x timeout +
//        judge_timeout) — large, but finite.
//     2. `max_iterations 10` caps how many passes can happen at all.
//     3. `gaviero-cli --run-timeout <secs>` caps the whole run. It
//        stops between agents and keeps everything written so far, so
//        re-running resumes rather than restarting.
//
//   A reviewer that blows its budget becomes a Failed manifest, which
//   the delivery gate then turns into a clean abort naming it — the
//   run stops with a diagnosis instead of hanging.
//
// Stopping early on a deadlock:
//
//   `irreconcilable_after 2` — a panel can disagree for reasons no
//   further iteration will resolve: the reviewers agree on the
//   evidence and still differ on values, or the deciding experiment
//   has not been run. Two mechanisms catch that, and either stops the
//   loop and writes {{OUT_DIR}}/consensus-irreconcilable.md — one
//   section per reviewer, giving its position, who it conflicts with,
//   which convergence criterion is at stake, and what evidence would
//   settle it:
//
//     - The judge returns `"verdict": "irreconcilable"` with a
//       `blockers` array (see the judge prompt for the schema and the
//       test it applies).
//     - The runtime notices the judge reporting the *same* blocking
//       disagreement twice running, whatever verdict it returns.
//       Fingerprinting is on the blockers' agent/criterion pairing,
//       not the prose, so rephrasing does not reset the count.
//
//   One case skips the wait: a panel that is not studying one
//   subject, or that reports the anchor as unusable, is a broken
//   input rather than a disagreement, so the judge is told to return
//   `irreconcilable` on the FIRST iteration where it appears (see
//   "Anchor precedence"). `irreconcilable_after` still governs every
//   genuine deadlock.
//
//   This is the failure-side mirror of `stability 2`: two identical
//   FAILs mean the disagreement is structural, exactly as two
//   consecutive PASSes mean the agreement is real. Set it to 0 to
//   disable and let the panel run to `max_iterations`.
//
// Conclusion / summary contract:
//   - conclusion: standalone hand-off document, complete on its own —
//     no peer ids, no version numbers, no iteration narrative, and no
//     reference to any other document. Its only external references
//     are the sources in its own `## Bibliography` section.
//   - summary: how that conclusion was produced. Every bit of history
//     lives here — convergence status, problems found, comparison
//     findings, what changed, what was rejected, the literature
//     research performed this pass, remaining research questions. The
//     judge's cheap path reads only summaries.
//
// Inputs (set via --var):
//   PLAN_FILE     — the anchor document: a problem statement or a
//                   draft research plan (see above). This is the
//                   run's only source of truth — see "Anchor
//                   precedence". (default: research_problem.md)
//   OUT_DIR       — directory for all generated files. (default:
//                   research; plan-anchored runs default it to the
//                   plan's own folder instead)
//   DATA_DIR      — optional local data directory, read-only for every
//                   reviewer. May be empty or non-existent. (default: data)
//   LOCAL_LIT_DIR — optional local literature directory, read-only for
//                   every reviewer. May be empty. (default: literature)
//   SEMANTIC_SCHOLAR_SERVER  — MCP server id agents call tools on.
//                   (default: semantic-scholar)
//   SEMANTIC_SCHOLAR_MCP_URL — remote MCP URL for operator setup; may
//                   also come from --mcp-url or .cursor/mcp.json.
//   ENABLE_WEB_FALLBACK — allow WebSearch/WebFetch. (default: true;
//                   set false for an MCP-only panel)
//
// Cost note:
//   Multiple frontier providers x up to 10 refinement passes plus init
//   is expensive — easily 30+ deep-reasoning turns for a 3-reviewer
//   panel. Convergence via the judge is the only way to exit early;
//   the prompts deliberately push reviewers to declare consensus
//   honestly once substantive disagreements close. `stability 2`
//   guards against a flaky single PASS verdict.
//
// Consensus policy (strict only):
//
//   `consensus_mode strict` — the loop exits only on judge PASS (two
//   consecutive passes via `stability 2`). There is no partial_ok exit.
//   A single run already retries refinement up to `max_iterations 10`;
//   you do not need to invoke the workflow twice in one session for that.
//
//   If the run ends without consensus, read each reviewer's latest
//   `*-summary-v*.md` — when status is `Consensus not reached.` the
//   summary must explain why (see refine prompt). Then either accept
//   the best-effort conclusions for human merge, or re-run the same
//   command to carry the panel further.
//
//   Re-running against the same OUT_DIR **resumes**: the runtime finds
//   the newest version every reviewer completed and continues at the
//   next one, so the init round is skipped and round N+1 reads round
//   N's conclusions. `max_iterations` is the budget for the new run,
//   counted from the resumed start. A round that only some reviewers
//   finished is discarded and re-run in full. Pass `--fresh` to ignore
//   what is on disk and restart the panel from `iter_start` instead;
//   use a new `--var OUT_DIR=...` when the {{PLAN_FILE}} or roster
//   changed, since those artefacts answer a different question.
//
// Usage:
//
//   Research mode — default 3-provider panel:
//     gaviero-cli --script crates/gaviero-dsl/examples/scientific_research.gaviero \
//       --workflow scientific-research-consensus \
//       --workspace /path/to/research-workspace \
//       --prompt "Short topic title for logs" \
//       --var PLAN_FILE=research_problem.md \
//       --var OUT_DIR=research
//
//   Plan-refinement mode — no --workspace; artefacts land next to the plan:
//     gaviero-cli --script crates/gaviero-dsl/examples/scientific_research.gaviero \
//       --workflow scientific-research-consensus \
//       --prompt "Sparse attention mechanisms" \
//       --var PLAN_FILE=/path/to/draft-research-plan.md \
//       --var SEMANTIC_SCHOLAR_MCP_URL=https://YOUR-MCP-ENDPOINT/ \
//       --mcp-codex-trust granted
//
//   Custom 2-provider panel:
//     gaviero-cli --script crates/gaviero-dsl/examples/scientific_research.gaviero \
//       --workflow scientific-research-consensus \
//       --workspace /path/to/research-workspace \
//       --prompt "Short topic title for logs" \
//       --param roster=claude=claude:opus@xhigh,codex=codex:gpt-5.6-sol@xhigh

// ── Shared configuration ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// PLAN_FILE: the external anchor document — the run's only source of
// truth. {{PROMPT}} from the CLI is a non-authoritative label; see
// "Anchor precedence" in the header.
//
// DATA_DIR / LOCAL_LIT_DIR: optional local context. When the directories
// don't exist or are empty, agents fall back to literature research +
// the anchor. The defaults match the conventional layout but are fully
// overridable.
//
// INIT_VER: the version written by the init phase; read by the first
// loop pass as PREV_ITER (iter_start 2 -> PREV_ITER=1 on iteration 1).

vars {
    OUT_DIR                   "research"
    INIT_VER                  "1"
    PLAN_FILE                 "research_problem.md"
    DATA_DIR                  "data"
    LOCAL_LIT_DIR             "literature"
    SEMANTIC_SCHOLAR_SERVER   "semantic-scholar"
    SEMANTIC_SCHOLAR_MCP_URL  ""
    ENABLE_WEB_FALLBACK       "true"
}

// ── Prompts ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Three prompts:
//   - init-body:     independent first pass, conclusion + summary
//   - refine-body:   peer-aware refinement, conclusion + summary
//   - judge-body:    two-pass convergence judgement
//
// All three reference {{REVIEWER_ID}} (the roster entry id),
// {{PEER_IDS}} / {{PEER_COUNT}} / {{PEER_READ_BLOCK}} (peer enumeration
// auto-substituted by the roster expansion), and the runtime
// {{ITER}} / {{PREV_ITER}} / {{ITER_EVIDENCE}} substitutions.

prompt init-body #"
    You are an independent senior scientific research strategist.

    Run label (NOT a source of truth): {{PROMPT}}
    Anchor document — the subject of this study: {{PLAN_FILE}}

    Precedence, before anything else. {{PLAN_FILE}} defines what is
    being studied. The run label above is a short human-readable
    string for logs and filenames only; it carries no authority, it
    may be stale, and it may name something else entirely. If the two
    appear to name different subjects, the anchor wins — without
    exception. Never adopt the label's subject, never blend the two,
    and never read the label as a hint about scope.

    Research channels:
      - Semantic Scholar MCP server id: {{SEMANTIC_SCHOLAR_SERVER}}
        (operator-configured; remote URL when set: {{SEMANTIC_SCHOLAR_MCP_URL}})
      - Web fallback (WebSearch/WebFetch) enabled: {{ENABLE_WEB_FALLBACK}}

    Optional local material:
      - Data:              {{DATA_DIR}}/
      - Local literature:  {{LOCAL_LIT_DIR}}/
    These directories may not exist or may be empty — treat them as
    optional. When absent, focus on literature research and the anchor.

    1. Read {{PLAN_FILE}} in full. Treat it as the authoritative
       description of the endeavour and decide which kind of anchor
       it is:
         - a *problem statement* (available data, existing results,
           constraints, what success looks like): build the research
           programme from it;
         - a *draft research plan* (it already proposes hypotheses,
           methodology, milestones): treat your conclusion as that
           plan's refined successor — strengthen its hypotheses,
           methodology, evidence bars, milestones, and stopping rules
           rather than answering from scratch.
       Either way, do NOT paraphrase loosely; every downstream
       conclusion you draw must trace back to something in that file.

       If the anchor turns out to be unusable — it falls outside what
       a scientific research study can address, it asks only for an
       artefact the Hard rules below forbid, or it is too ambiguous
       to yield a research question — then that is your finding, and
       reporting it is the deliverable. Still write both files: name
       the defect as the FIRST item of the summary's
       `## Problems found`, state precisely what the operator must
       change in {{PLAN_FILE}}, and give the best analysis you can of
       the subject the anchor actually names. Do NOT fall back to the
       run label's subject, and do NOT invent a research question the
       anchor does not contain. A broken anchor is a defect for the
       operator to fix, never a gap for you to fill.

    2. Inspect {{DATA_DIR}}/ and {{LOCAL_LIT_DIR}}/ for relevant local
       material. Treat any local data as evidence to be characterised,
       not as proof. If a directory does not exist, skip it.

    3. Do NOT read any peer outputs (other reviewers' conclusion /
       summary files) under {{OUT_DIR}}/. This is the
       independent first pass; peer analyses arrive only in the refine
       phase. Reading {{PLAN_FILE}} is fine even when it lives
       inside {{OUT_DIR}}/.

    4. Conduct EXTENSIVE current literature research. Prefer the
       Semantic Scholar MCP server ({{SEMANTIC_SCHOLAR_SERVER}}) when
       its tools are available; use WebSearch/WebFetch when
       ENABLE_WEB_FALLBACK=true (current: {{ENABLE_WEB_FALLBACK}}) or
       when no MCP server is configured. Prioritise:
         - peer-reviewed papers, preprints, datasets, benchmarks,
           standards, and reputable technical reports;
         - literature from the last 24–36 months when the field is
           active; include seminal older work only where it materially
           changes the analysis;
         - leading research groups, dominant techniques, recent
           surprises, and the open questions;
         - negative results, reproducibility issues, retractions, and
           evidence that weakens attractive hypotheses;
         - the citation graph two hops deep where something seems
           load-bearing.

       Do NOT skip this step. The workflow's whole point is that each
       provider brings a fresh, well-sourced literature view;
       superficial research undermines the comparison phase.

    5. Think from first principles AFTER the research, not before. The
       literature constrains what's been tried and what failed;
       first-principles thinking constrains what's plausible under the
       {{PLAN_FILE}} constraints. Combine them.

    Hard rules:
      - Be critical, honest, and specific. State your real best
        assessment, including where you think the problem may be
        ill-posed or where the available data is likely insufficient.
      - Distinguish what is known from what is inferred.
      - Do NOT invent citations, datasets, or results. If no research
        channel is available, state that explicitly in the summary
        and rely only on local material.
      - Do NOT optimise for agreement; optimise for truth and for
        useful next research actions.
      - The scientific topic has no relationship to whatever
        repository or folder hosts this run unless the anchor itself
        says so — do not read or search the host checkout beyond the
        inputs listed above.
      - Do NOT produce an implementation roadmap (no code modules,
        PR phases, or software-project decomposition) — this holds
        for draft-plan anchors too.
      - {{PLAN_FILE}} is the only thing that fixes the subject. If
        you find yourself analysing something the anchor does not
        describe, stop and re-read it. An anchor you cannot use is a
        reportable defect, not a licence to pick your own subject.

    Write TWO files. Create each exactly once with the version suffix
    below; never edit or overwrite a file from a prior iteration or
    peer.

    File 1 — the conclusion:
      {{OUT_DIR}}/{{REVIEWER_ID}}-conclusion-v{{INIT_VER}}.md

    It must contain exactly these sections, in this order:
      - `## Problem understanding`
      - `## Current evidence base`
      - `## Best avenues for advance`
      - `## Critical risks and failure modes`
      - `## Data and literature gaps`
      - `## Proposed research programme`
      - `## Decision points and stopping criteria`
      - `## Bibliography`

    Requirements for File 1:
      - The document must stand entirely on its own: a reader with
        only this file (and the sources its bibliography points to)
        has everything. No pointers to any other document.
      - Rank the best avenues for advance.
      - For each avenue, state the central hypothesis, evidence
        needed, expected upside, main risks, first decisive experiment
        or analysis, and stopping criteria.
      - Include explicit reasons why attractive avenues may fail.
      - Decision points and stopping criteria must be specific enough
        to act on — e.g. "abandon if SNR < 3σ on dataset X" rather
        than "abandon if results are weak".
      - `## Bibliography` lists every source the document relies on:
        title, authors (when available), venue/source, year/date, URL
        or DOI. Mark each as `local`, `web`, `peer-reviewed`,
        `preprint`, `dataset`, `standard`, or `report` when possible.
        Every claim in the body must trace to the anchor, local
        material, or a bibliography entry.

    File 2 — the production summary:
      {{OUT_DIR}}/{{REVIEWER_ID}}-summary-v{{INIT_VER}}.md

    This is the log of how File 1 was produced. It must contain
    exactly these sections, in this order:
      - `## How this version was produced` — state that this is the
        independent first pass (no peer input), which anchor kind you
        detected, and how you approached it.
      - `## Search strategy` — the search queries, Semantic Scholar
        MCP tool calls, and discovery paths used, and which channels
        were actually available.
      - `## Problems found` — where the anchor is ambiguous,
        ill-posed, or under-specified; claims you could not verify;
        evidence that surprised you.
      - `## Research limitations` — what you could not cover and why.

    Aim for depth over breadth. A 3000-word conclusion engaging
    seriously with 15 sources beats a 1500-word conclusion name-dropping
    50.
"#

prompt refine-body #"
    You are refining your independent scientific research conclusion.

    Run label (NOT a source of truth): {{PROMPT}}
    Anchor document — the subject of this study: {{PLAN_FILE}}
    Optional local material: {{DATA_DIR}}/ and {{LOCAL_LIT_DIR}}/

    Precedence, before anything else. {{PLAN_FILE}} defines what is
    being studied. The run label above is a short human-readable
    string for logs and filenames only; it carries no authority, it
    may be stale, and it may name something else entirely. If the two
    appear to name different subjects, the anchor wins — without
    exception. Never adopt the label's subject, never blend the two,
    and never read the label as a hint about scope.

    Research channels:
      - Semantic Scholar MCP server id: {{SEMANTIC_SCHOLAR_SERVER}}
      - Web fallback (WebSearch/WebFetch) enabled: {{ENABLE_WEB_FALLBACK}}

    There are {{PEER_COUNT}} other reviewers in this iteration: {{PEER_IDS}}.
    Your reviewer id is {{REVIEWER_ID}}.

    Inputs to read this pass:

    1. Re-read {{PLAN_FILE}} in full. The anchor is the source of
       truth — every conclusion must trace back to a constraint,
       datum, or goal listed there.

       Every reviewer on this panel was given the same anchor, so all
       of you are studying the same subject. If a peer's conclusion
       is about something the anchor does not describe — the run
       label's subject, say — that is a DEFECT in that peer's output,
       not a position to be adjudicated. Record it under
       `## Merits of each proposal`, keep your own analysis anchored,
       and do not move toward it; a panel cannot converge by picking
       a subject. Likewise, if the anchor itself is unusable (see the
       init instructions), every reviewer should be reporting that
       same defect rather than each choosing a different way around
       it — state the defect and what the operator must change, and
       do not negotiate a substitute subject with your peers.

    2. Read your own previous work:
         - {{OUT_DIR}}/{{REVIEWER_ID}}-conclusion-v{{PREV_ITER}}.md
         - {{OUT_DIR}}/{{REVIEWER_ID}}-summary-v{{PREV_ITER}}.md
       (Every iteration, including the v1 init pass, writes both.)

    3. Read every peer's previous work IN FULL.

{{PEER_READ_BLOCK}}

       Do read peer summaries even for v1 — the init pass writes one
       too, and it carries that peer's search trail and the problems
       they found. A peer's sources live in their conclusion's
       `## Bibliography` and their summary's research sections; use
       them to verify the claims behind each peer's argument.

    4. Conduct ADDITIONAL targeted literature research this iteration
       through the same channels (Semantic Scholar MCP first; web
       tools per the fallback rules above). Do NOT merely repeat the
       searches from the previous pass. Specifically:
         - Read primary sources peers cited that you did not. If a peer
           grounds a claim in a paper outside your prior bibliography,
           fetch and read that paper before evaluating their argument.
         - Look for newer publications (preprints in the last six
           months — the field moves fast).
         - Search for counter-evidence to the strongest claims in your
           previous conclusion. Specifically try to falsify your own
           strongest conclusion.
         - Follow up on any "I'd want to check X" notes from prior
           summaries.

       Each refinement pass MUST be backed by fresh searches, not just
       re-reading the prior documents. A refinement that adds no new
       sources adds no new signal — the whole point of iterating is to
       deepen the evidence base.

    Then synthesise:

    5. Evaluate every peer's conclusion on its merits. For each peer
       in {{PEER_IDS}}:
         - Strongest claim — and whether the literature supports it.
         - Weakest claim — and what evidence would change your mind.
         - What this peer saw that you did not (techniques, risks,
           sources, framings).
         - What this peer overlooked or got wrong, citing sources.

    6. Decide whether the panel has converged. Convergence means every
       reviewer's analysis agrees on:
         - the framing of the problem;
         - the top two or three avenues for advance (the same
           directions, not necessarily the same priority ordering);
         - the dominant risks and failure modes;
         - the evidence required to validate or falsify the top
           avenues;
         - the decision points and stopping criteria.

       Editorial differences (wording, section length, which extra
       sources are cited beyond a shared core) DO NOT block convergence.

         - **If the panel has converged**: do NOT introduce new
           directions, risks, or analytical frames just to make
           progress visible. Copy your previous conclusion
           ({{OUT_DIR}}/{{REVIEWER_ID}}-conclusion-v{{PREV_ITER}}.md)
           into v{{ITER}} verbatim — same sections, same prose, same
           bibliography. Byte stability across iterations is the
           desired outcome. Declare convergence in the summary (step
           8). Do not converge for social reasons.

         - **If the panel has NOT converged**: synthesise a new
           conclusion taking the strongest evidence-backed elements
           from your prior conclusion AND from the peer conclusions,
           and resolve conflicts on their merits. Make the minimum
           changes needed to close the actual gaps — do not invent
           additional refinements beyond what the disagreements demand.

    Outputs to write this pass — exactly TWO files. Write NEW
    versioned paths for this iteration only (`-v{{ITER}}`); never
    modify files from earlier iterations (including your own).

    7. The conclusion:
       {{OUT_DIR}}/{{REVIEWER_ID}}-conclusion-v{{ITER}}.md

       Exactly these sections, in this order:
         - `## Problem understanding`
         - `## Current evidence base`
         - `## Best avenues for advance`
         - `## Critical risks and failure modes`
         - `## Data and literature gaps`
         - `## Proposed research programme`
         - `## Decision points and stopping criteria`
         - `## Bibliography`

       Same shape as the init body — this is the document a human
       reviewer would read at the end of the run. Hard rules — failing
       any of these makes the conclusion unfit for hand-off:
         - Present your current best conclusion as a standalone
           document, complete without reading anything else: no
           pointers to other files, no assumed context beyond the
           anchor. Rank the avenues for advance. For each avenue,
           state hypothesis, evidence needed, expected upside, main
           risks, first decisive experiment or analysis, and stopping
           criteria.
         - `## Bibliography` is the ONLY place external references
           live: every source the body relies on, with title, authors
           (when available), venue/source, year/date, URL or DOI, and
           a `local` / `web` / `peer-reviewed` / `preprint` /
           `dataset` / `standard` / `report` mark when possible.
           Carry forward the bibliography entries that still back the
           document — the previous version's bibliography is your
           starting point, not a reference target.
         - Do NOT write "I previously said X but now…", "peer Y
           proposed Z which I reject…", or any first-person iteration
           narrative.
         - Do NOT reference peer ids, version numbers (v{{PREV_ITER}},
           v{{ITER}}), or refinement passes anywhere in the conclusion
           body.
         - Do NOT include `## Synthesis Notes`, `## Comparison`, or
           any other iteration-metadata section in the conclusion.

       All iteration metadata, peer comparison, and version references
       belong in the summary file (step 8) — never the conclusion.

    8. The production summary:
       {{OUT_DIR}}/{{REVIEWER_ID}}-summary-v{{ITER}}.md

       Everything about HOW this version came to be lives here and
       only here: the iteration history, the problems found, what was
       agreed and what was not, and the research that backed it. Keep
       it under ~1200 words, with the consensus sections first and
       tight — the judge's cheap pass reads only the top of this file.
       Use these sections, in this order:
         - `## Consensus status` — the very first sentence MUST be
           EXACTLY one of these two strings, with no other content
           before it:
              `Consensus reached.`
              `Consensus not reached.`
           The judge keys off that exact phrasing for its cheap
           summary-only pass. If this version is a no-material-change
           convergence copy, write `Consensus reached.` and add a
           second sentence stating which previous version you copied
           verbatim (e.g. "Copied conclusion-v{{PREV_ITER}} into
           v{{ITER}} unchanged."). Otherwise write `Consensus not
           reached.` and one sentence naming the dominant blocking
           disagreement (avenue, risk, evidence bar, or stopping rule).
         - `## Why consensus was not reached` — REQUIRED when the first
           sentence is `Consensus not reached.`; omit this section only
           when the first sentence is `Consensus reached.`. Explain in
           plain language: (a) which convergence criterion from step 6
           failed (framing, top avenues, risks, evidence required, or
           stopping criteria); (b) which peer positions conflict and
           why the literature does not yet adjudicate them; (c) what
           evidence or problem clarification would unblock consensus.
           This section is the human handoff when the workflow stops
           without a judge PASS — be specific, not generic.
         - `## Merits of each proposal` — one short subsection per
           peer id in {{PEER_IDS}}. For each: what you adopted from
           them, what you rejected, what specific evidence drove the
           adjudication. Cite sources.
         - `## Substantive disagreements` — disagreements that are
           about substance (avenues, risks, evidence required,
           stopping criteria), not wording. Empty list if converged.
         - `## Changes made in this iteration` — what you changed in
           your conclusion this pass and why. If you copied the
           previous version verbatim, say so here too.
         - `## Research performed this iteration` — the search
           queries, Semantic Scholar MCP tool calls, and discovery
           paths used this pass; the new or revalidated sources (one
           line each: title, venue, year, URL or DOI) and which claim
           each supports or weakens; which prior claims this pass
           strengthened or weakened; and any channel limitations. If
           the panel converged and you ran only verification searches,
           say so in one line.
         - `## Remaining research questions` — the disagreements you
           expect the next iteration to address. Empty list if
           converged.

       Write the summary for a human skimming the iteration evolution.
"#

prompt judge-body #"
    You are deciding whether a panel of independently refined scientific
    research conclusions has reached consensus for:

    Run label (NOT a source of truth): {{PROMPT}}
    Anchor document — the subject under study: {{PLAN_FILE}}

    The anchor defines the subject. The run label is a short string
    for logs; it carries no authority and may name something else
    entirely. A reviewer whose conclusion studies the label's
    subject — or any subject the anchor does not describe — has
    produced a DEFECTIVE artefact, not a competing position. Never
    score that as a substantive disagreement for further iterations
    to resolve; see the IRRECONCILABLE rule below.

    {{ITER_EVIDENCE}}

    The digest above lists what the refine agents actually changed
    this iteration. Use it to focus your reading — if a file is not
    listed there it did not change this pass.

    Evaluate in two passes — start cheap, only escalate to the full
    conclusion documents on borderline calls. Reading every conclusion
    for every reviewer each iteration is expensive; the summaries
    exist precisely so this pass can usually settle on summaries alone.

    Pass 1 (cheap, always do this):

    1. Read every reviewer's summary for iteration {{ITER}}. The files
       live under {{OUT_DIR}}/ and match the glob:
         {{OUT_DIR}}/*-summary-v{{ITER}}.md
       Each summary is ≤ ~1200 words with the consensus sections at
       the top; it carries the convergence status, comparison
       findings, changes, the research log, and remaining open
       questions for this pass. You normally only need the top
       consensus sections — the trailing research log is for peers
       and the human handoff.

    2. From the summaries alone, classify the iteration (`pass`,
       `fail`, or `irreconcilable` — never partial):

       - **PASS**: EVERY summary's first sentence is exactly
         `Consensus reached.`. A single `Consensus not reached.`
         blocks PASS. When all declare reached, return PASS even if
         `## Changes made in this iteration` notes a verbatim copy or
         minor editorial tweaks — those do not negate consensus.

       - **FAIL**: ANY summary's first sentence is exactly
         `Consensus not reached.`, AND the disagreement still looks
         closable by another round of evidence. Read that reviewer's
         `## Why consensus was not reached` (and
         `## Substantive disagreements`) for the reason; you do not
         need Pass 2 unless summaries are contradictory or evasive.

       - **IRRECONCILABLE**: consensus was not reached AND another
         round would not change that. Return this — do not spend the
         panel's remaining budget — when any of these hold:

           * the same substantive disagreement appears in this
             iteration and in v{{PREV_ITER}}, restated rather than
             narrowed (compare the `## Substantive disagreements`
             sections of both);
           * the reviewers agree on the evidence and still reach
             opposite conclusions — the conflict is about values,
             risk tolerance, or interpretation, not about facts;
           * the evidence that would settle it does not exist yet
             (an unrun experiment, an unpublished dataset), so no
             amount of further literature search can adjudicate it;
           * the anchor document is ambiguous on the exact point in
             dispute, so the panel is arguing about the question
             rather than the answer;
           * **the panel is not studying one subject** — reviewers
             analysed different endeavours, or one or more reported
             the anchor as unusable, out of scope, or too ambiguous
             to yield a research question. This is a broken input,
             and no iteration repairs a broken input. Return
             IRRECONCILABLE the FIRST time it appears — do not wait
             for it to repeat — with `criterion` set to `framing`
             and `evidence_gap` naming exactly what the operator must
             change in {{PLAN_FILE}} or in the run's parameters.
             Emit one blocker per affected reviewer so the hand-off
             document names them.

         Being reluctant to declare this is expensive: every extra
         iteration re-derives the same deadlock at frontier-model
         rates. Being hasty is also expensive — a disagreement that
         is merely *unresolved after one pass* is a FAIL, not an
         impasse. The distinguishing test is whether you can name the
         specific evidence that would settle it and whether that
         evidence is reachable this run.

       - **Borderline**: first sentences disagree with body content,
         or some summaries lack `## Why consensus was not reached`
         when required — continue to Pass 2.

    Pass 2 (deep, only on borderline):

    3. Read every reviewer's conclusion for iteration {{ITER}}:
         {{OUT_DIR}}/*-conclusion-v{{ITER}}.md

    4. If you still need it, compare against the v{{PREV_ITER}}
       conclusions to judge whether a specific difference is material.

    5. Return PASS if the conclusions substantively agree on:
         - the ranked best avenues for advance;
         - the critical risks and failure modes;
         - the evidence required to validate or falsify the top
           avenues;
         - the data and literature gaps that matter most;
         - the decision points and stopping criteria.
       Wording differences and extra citations beyond a shared core
       DO NOT block convergence — they are expected artefacts of
       independent literature reviews.

    6. Otherwise return FAIL, or IRRECONCILABLE if the step-2 test for
       a structural deadlock applies.

    Output format — emit exactly one fenced JSON object as your last
    output. Keep `reason` terse (one short sentence) so the response
    fits inside the judge timeout:

    ```json
    {"verdict": "pass" | "fail" | "irreconcilable",
     "reason": "<one short sentence>",
     "blockers": []}
    ```

    `blockers` is REQUIRED whenever the verdict is `fail` or
    `irreconcilable`, and must contain one entry per reviewer that
    holds a blocking position — the runtime writes the human hand-off
    document straight from these fields, and it also compares them
    across iterations to detect a disagreement that is merely being
    restated. Keep the wording of `criterion` and `conflicts_with`
    stable between iterations when the underlying dispute has not
    changed; that stability is the signal:

    ```json
    {"verdict": "irreconcilable",
     "reason": "Panel splits on whether executable correctness gates release.",
     "blockers": [
       {"agent": "claude",
        "position": "Executable correctness is a necessary release gate.",
        "conflicts_with": "codex",
        "criterion": "evidence bar",
        "evidence_gap": "No study measures honest-student outcomes under perturbation."}
     ]}
    ```

    Use exactly one of `framing`, `avenues`, `risks`, `evidence bar`,
    or `stopping criteria` for `criterion` — these are the convergence
    criteria the reviewers were given. `agent` must be a reviewer id
    exactly as it appears in the artefact filenames.

    A plain `VERDICT: PASS` / `VERDICT: FAIL` line is accepted as a
    fallback, but it carries no blockers, so the hand-off document
    would be empty — always prefer the JSON form.
"#

// ── Agents ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Two reviewer templates (init and refine) plus a fixed convergence
// judge. The init / refine templates are cloned per roster entry by
// the loop expansion in crates/gaviero-dsl/src/reviewers.rs:
//
//   - {{REVIEWER_ID}} is substituted with each entry's id in the
//     description, scope paths, and prompt body.
//   - Each clone is bound to an anonymous client carrying that entry's
//     model + effort.
//   - Refine clones additionally get {{PEER_IDS}}, {{PEER_COUNT}}, and
//     {{PEER_READ_BLOCK}} substituted for cross-reviewer prompting.
//
// The `tools` list covers the web fallback channel only; Semantic
// Scholar arrives through the MCP configuration (see header), not
// through this list.
//
// Init agents deliberately do NOT have {{OUT_DIR}}/ in their read_only
// scope — they form an independent baseline and the prompt forbids
// reading peer outputs. Refine agents do read {{OUT_DIR}}/.

agent reviewer-init {
    template true
    description "Initial scientific research analysis ({{REVIEWER_ID}}): {{PROMPT}}"
    tools ["WebSearch" "WebFetch"]
    scope {
        owned [
            "{{OUT_DIR}}/{{REVIEWER_ID}}-conclusion-v*.md"
            "{{OUT_DIR}}/{{REVIEWER_ID}}-summary-v*.md"
        ]
        read_only [
            "{{PLAN_FILE}}"
            "{{DATA_DIR}}/"
            "{{LOCAL_LIT_DIR}}/"
        ]
    }
    produces [
        "{{OUT_DIR}}/{{REVIEWER_ID}}-conclusion-v{{INIT_VER}}.md"
        "{{OUT_DIR}}/{{REVIEWER_ID}}-summary-v{{INIT_VER}}.md"
    ]
    prompt init-body
    max_retries 1
    // Deep literature research legitimately runs long; this is the
    // liveness backstop, not a target. See the header "Termination".
    timeout 3600
}

agent reviewer-refine {
    template true
    description "Refine scientific research conclusion ({{REVIEWER_ID}}): {{PROMPT}}"
    tools ["WebSearch" "WebFetch"]
    scope {
        owned [
            "{{OUT_DIR}}/{{REVIEWER_ID}}-conclusion-v*.md"
            "{{OUT_DIR}}/{{REVIEWER_ID}}-summary-v*.md"
        ]
        read_only [
            "{{PLAN_FILE}}"
            "{{DATA_DIR}}/"
            "{{LOCAL_LIT_DIR}}/"
            "{{OUT_DIR}}/"
        ]
    }
    produces [
        "{{OUT_DIR}}/{{REVIEWER_ID}}-conclusion-v{{ITER}}.md"
        "{{OUT_DIR}}/{{REVIEWER_ID}}-summary-v{{ITER}}.md"
    ]
    prompt refine-body
    max_retries 1
    timeout 3600
    memory {
        write_ns   "scientific-research-consensus"
        importance 0.8
        write_content #"
            Scientific research refinement for {{PROMPT}} anchored to {{PLAN_FILE}}.
        "#
    }
}

agent convergence-judge {
    description "Judge consensus across reviewer panel: {{PROMPT}}"
    client judge
    scope {
        read_only ["{{OUT_DIR}}/" "{{PLAN_FILE}}"]
    }
    prompt judge-body
    max_retries 1
}

// ── Workflow ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The `roster` workflow parameter binds the loop's `reviewers` slot to
// a list of provider entries. Override entirely from the CLI:
//
//   --param roster=claude=claude:opus@xhigh,codex=codex:gpt-5.6-sol@xhigh,cursor=cursor:grok-4.5@xhigh
//
// The in-script default below runs that same three-provider frontier
// panel (Claude Opus + Codex GPT-5.6 Sol + Cursor Grok 4.5) so the
// example works with no flag. The Cursor entry's `effort` is a no-op —
// the Cursor backend does not forward it (see header roster note).
//
// `execution_mode document` (see header) is declared on this workflow:
// refine agents must read peer artefacts under {{OUT_DIR}}/, which
// per-agent git worktrees would hide until after the loop exits.
//
// Loop knobs:
//
//   stability 2       — require 2 consecutive PASS verdicts before
//                       exiting. A single PASS is treated as
//                       provisional; the refine agents run one more
//                       pass and the judge re-evaluates. Guards
//                       against a flaky "looks good" verdict on a
//                       panel that is still materially evolving.
//   judge_timeout 240 — hard cap each judge invocation at 240s (0
//                       disables). Wide enough that even the deep-read
//                       borderline path covering every reviewer's
//                       conclusion can finish.
//   strict_judge false — unparseable judge output is treated as FAIL
//                       rather than a hard error; a timeout on the
//                       final loop check cannot bury the run.
//   iter_start 2      — loop iteration 1 -> ITER=2, PREV_ITER=1
//                       (reads the v1 init files); subsequent passes
//                       increment by 1 up to ITER=11 at iteration 10.
//
// The judge prompt also uses {{ITER_EVIDENCE}}: the runtime substitutes
// a bounded digest of the refine agents' most recent summaries and
// modified files so the judge can focus on what actually changed.
//
// Post-run artefacts:
//
//   On consensus: one conclusion + summary pair per reviewer for the
//   iteration that converged. None is canonical — the operator picks
//   one or merges.
//
//   Without consensus: the loop stops after `max_iterations` or a
//   failed final judge check. Use the highest `*-summary-v{{ITER}}.md`
//   per reviewer; `Consensus not reached.` entries must document why
//   in `## Why consensus was not reached`. A new CLI run is optional
//   (see header); this script does not auto-restart.
//
// max_parallel 5 supports rosters up to five reviewers running in
// parallel; the default 3-reviewer panel uses three of those slots.

workflow scientific-research-consensus {
    execution_mode document
    param judge {
        model "claude:sonnet"
        effort medium
    }
    param roster [
        { id "claude" model "claude:opus"       effort xhigh }
        { id "codex"  model "codex:gpt-5.6-sol" effort xhigh }
        { id "cursor" model "cursor:grok-4.5"   effort xhigh }
    ]

    steps [
        loop {
            reviewers       roster
            template_init   reviewer-init
            template_refine reviewer-refine
            max_iterations  10
            iter_start      2
            stability       2
            judge_timeout   240
            strict_judge    false
            consensus_mode  strict
            irreconcilable_after 2
            until agent     convergence-judge
        }
    ]
    max_parallel 5
    memory {
        write_ns "scientific-research-consensus"
    }
}
